WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:05.840
It’s crazy when you think about all of
the different kinds of foods we eat.

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:09.760
We just swallow and hope
it all works out for the best.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:11.280
Well, as it turns out,

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:15.680
there are better ways to think about keeping
our bodies humming healthfully along.

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:18.080
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast,

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:21.200
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:28.480
How does eating meat or poultry contribute
to the development of antibiotic resistance?

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:34.000
Here's our first story with NutritionFacts
Senior Research Scientist, Dr. Kristine Dennis.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.160
According to
the World Health Organization,

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:38.240
more antibiotics
are fed to farm animals

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:40.640
to prevent disease
and fatten them faster

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:42.960
than are used
to treat sick people.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:46.240
This can foster the growth of
antibiotic-resistant superbugs

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:48.960
that can then get
transmitted to humans.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:51.280
As the UK's
Chief Medical Officer

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:52.720
once put it,

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:54.880
"Every inappropriate
or unnecessary use

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:56.640
in animals or agriculture

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:58.480
is potentially signing
a death warrant

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:00.320
for a future patient.”

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:04.560
So, what are they:
miracle drugs or pig food?

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:06.400
Here's how Dr. Greger
framed the issue

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:09.200
in a debate he had
with industry representatives,

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:14.640
including from the
National Pork Producers Council.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:16.640
To ask if there's a question

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:20.560
between human drug resistance
and livestock antibiotic use

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:25.360
is like asking if there's
human-induced climate change,

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:30.160
or if there's a link
between smoking and lung cancer.

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:34.240
There are a few dissenting
industry scientists on one side,

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:35.360
but then, basically,

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:37.680
every other scientist
on the other.

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:40.560
Both opposing camps
have scientists on their side,

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:43.520
but only one really has
science on their side.

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:46.800
One can just look at who's
on the sides of this debate.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:47.680
In one corner,

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:49.200
you have
the World Health Organization,

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:50.800
the American Medical
Association,

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:52.320
the American Academy
of Pediatrics,

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:53.680
American Public Health
Association,

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:55.360
another 100

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:58.240
medical and public health
organizations in the country.

00:01:58.240 --> 00:01:59.440
On the other side,

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:02.080
you have
the National Turkey Federation,

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:04.000
the National Chicken Council,

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.240
the Sheep Industry Association,

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:08.000
the National Pork Producers.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:10.960
It could not be more stark.

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:12.720
Previously, Dr. Greger discussed

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:14.880
how feeding millions of pounds
of antibiotics

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:17.760
to cows, pigs, and chickens
every year is dangerous,

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:20.880
because it fosters
antibiotic resistance--

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:22.960
not necessarily
because there's a problem

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:26.720
with antibiotic drug residues
getting into the meat itself.

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:29.840
But, turns out,
he may be wrong.

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:32.400
In 2010,
the U.S. Inspector General

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:34.320
slammed the U.S.
Department of Agriculture

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:36.160
for not protecting
the American public

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:37.680
from the contamination of meat

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:41.040
with residual drugs,
pesticides, and heavy metals.

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:42.640
These drug and chemical residues

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:46.240
then find their way
to our dinner plates.

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:48.480
But in order to safeguard
the nation's food supply

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:50.000
from harmful residues,

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:51.040
the USDA,

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:52.400
Food and Drug Administration,

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:54.400
and Environmental
Protection Agency

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:56.880
are supposed to test
for these contaminants,

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:00.560
and prevent adulterated meat
from entering the food supply.

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:03.200
But, based on their review,
the U.S. Inspector General

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:04.880
found that
the National Residue Program

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:06.880
is not accomplishing its mission

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:09.920
of monitoring the food supply
for harmful residues.

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:13.120
Regulators hadn't even
established threshold values

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:15.280
for many dangerous substances,

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:17.280
which has resulted
in contaminated meat

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:19.520
being distributed to consumers.

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:21.200
What potential effects
could the drugs

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:22.480
and toxic metals in meat

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:24.560
have on people who consume it?

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:28.560
Gastrointestinal bleeding,
ulcers, allergic reactions,

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:31.600
serious nerve damage,
severe inflammation,

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:34.480
skin cancers,
internal cancers, jaundice,

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:38.800
kidney failure,
neurotoxicity, and even death.

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:41.760
Doesn't cooking
destroy the drugs, though?

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:42.800
No.

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:45.520
No amount of cooking
will destroy residues.

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:46.960
In fact, in some cases,

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:49.440
heat may actually
break residues down

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:53.680
into components that are even
more harmful to consumers.

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:55.520
The Pew Charitable Trusts
point out

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:57.920
that chemicals
that experts agree

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:00.000
pose a significant
public health hazard

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:02.560
are not routinely tested for,

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:04.400
while others
that pose little risk

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:07.280
are routinely included
in sampling plans.

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:10.800
The USDA does test
for some hazardous drugs though,

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:12.640
and found that only about

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:15.120
1 in 260 samples
of animal products

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:17.840
had drug residue violations.

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:21.120
But that suggests more than
100 million pounds of meat

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:22.640
adulterated with drugs

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:27.200
is served up every year
in the United States alone.

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:29.440
Did you know that
those eating plant-based

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:34.000
have a reduced load of antibiotic
resistance genes in their gut?

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:35.520
Here’s the story.

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:40.240
There are nearly a million salmonella
and campylobacter infections

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:44.080
in children 10 and younger
each year in the United States.

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:48.320
Some of these infections are severe,
causing meningitis and death, and

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:52.320
requiring treatment with antibiotics.
The problem is that there's

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:55.520
an increasing problem of
antibiotic resistance among

00:04:55.520 --> 00:04:58.720
these bugs that threatens
our ability to treat them.

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:02.960
Part of the problem is that the same
life-saving miracle-drug antibiotics

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:05.280
are being squandered
for use in food animals

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:07.040
for things like growth promotion

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:09.680
in such unhygienic,
crowded conditions,

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:12.560
which increases the likelihood
that pathogens like salmonella

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:14.800
or campylobacter will
become resistant,

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:17.280
and I've done a bunch
of videos on that.

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:21.440
But there's another problem.
The resistance determinants,

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:25.040
the genes that encode
antibiotic resistance,

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:29.200
may be transmitted from food animals
to humans through the food supply.

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:33.120
See, most resistant bacteria
have mobile genetic elements,

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:36.000
like these little circles
of DNA called plasmids,

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:39.120
that carry the resistance genes
that they can then pass on

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:43.440
to other bacteria, including
those in our own gut.

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:46.640
Food animals are, therefore,
a reservoir of antibiotic resistance

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:49.360
genes and a potential
vector for transmission

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:53.680
of antibiotic resistance genes to
the human intestinal microbiome.

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:57.200
In this study, transfer of an
antibiotic resistance plasmid

00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:00.320
from an E. coli originating
from a chicken raised for meat

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:03.680
to human gut bugs was
assessed by using a model

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:10.560
that mimics the human intestines.
And it happened within two hours.

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:14.800
This spread of antibiotic resistance
genes presents an alarming scenario,

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:18.880
a growing concern that antibiotic
resistant bacteria present on food

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:24.080
can transfer their resistance genes
to the inherent gut microbiome

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:25.760
of the consumer.

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:30.720
But you don't know
until you put it to the test.

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:37.040
Assessing antibiotic resistance
gene loads in vegan vs. vegetarian

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:40.720
vs. omnivore gut bacteria.

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:43.440
You'd think the results
might be obvious,

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:45.840
but antibiotic resistance
genes are spread due to

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:49.920
manure application on agricultural
fields of fruits and vegetables.

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:53.200
Yes, massive antibiotic use in
animal farming is considered

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:56.640
as the greatest contributor to the
presence of antibiotic-resistant

00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:00.560
bacteria in food of animal origin:
meats, eggs, and dairy.

00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:03.760
Nevertheless, sewage from
treated animals may impact

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:06.960
on vegetables grown
on fertilized fields,

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:10.080
but it was largely unknown
whether, and to what extent,

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:13.280
antibiotic-resistant bacteria are
being transferred to vegetables

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:18.080
and then to the human gut until now.

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:21.600
Researchers looked for antibiotic-
resistant genes against sulfa drugs

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:25.360
like bactrim, tetracyclines,
penicillins and cephalosporins,

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:27.920
and streptomycin-type antibiotics

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:32.240
and both omnivores and vegetarians
showed a signigicantly higher

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:34.880
antibiotic-resistant
gene load in their guts

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:37.120
as compared with vegans.

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:40.400
There wasn't a significant difference
between omnivores and vegetarians,

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:43.760
but significantly lower loads in
vegans compared to omnivores,

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:47.680
and vegans compared to
vegetarians, the first evidence

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:49.760
that a vegan lifestyle
is associated with

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:53.280
a reduced load of human
gut antibiotic-resistant genes,

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:54.800
but not the last.

00:07:54.800 --> 00:07:58.000
Fewer tetracycline resistance
genes in vegan guts and

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:02.320
more vancomycin resistance genes
in the guts of those who eat meat.

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:04.640
No surprise, since they found
a correlation between

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:06.320
tetracycline resistance genes

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:09.520
and the intake of eggs,
milk, and cheese ---

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:12.400
I like how there are so many
types of milk these days

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:15.600
they have to specify
"milk from animal sources."

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:18.800
And a higher incidence of
vancomycin resistance genes

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:22.480
was found in consumers
of eggs, poultry, fish, and seafood.

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:26.640
And vancomycin is one of our
antibiotics of last resort, used to

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:29.680
treat serious life-threatening
strep and staph infections

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:32.240
like MRSA (M-R-S-A).

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:35.120
Despite the links to dairy and
eggs, just cutting out meat

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:38.400
has indeed been shown to offer
an advantage in some studies,

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:42.720
as bacteria obtained from meat-eater
poop samples showed resistance

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:45.600
to a greater number of antibiotics
and carried more tested

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:52.000
antibiotic resistance genes compared
to the vegan or vegetarian poop.

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:52.880
Finally today,

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:59.040
we look at how tainted chicken may result in
more than a million urinary tract infections

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:01.840
in American women every year.

00:09:01.840 --> 00:09:06.240
In my video on antibiotic resistance
genes, I explored how more

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:08.960
were found in the guts of those
who eat meat, dairy, and eggs

00:09:08.960 --> 00:09:11.600
than those who eat
completely plant-based.

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:15.440
But does the transfer of bacteria
from animal foods in the human gut

00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:18.960
result in differences in
actual clinical outcomes --

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:21.120
other than food poisoning, of course?

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:25.440
Foodborne bacteria sicken about
350 million people every year,

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:29.200
and most of that can be traced
to meat, dairy products, and eggs.

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:34.000
Besides food poisoning, though,
what about extraintestinal infections,

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:38.960
infections outside the digestive tract,
for example, due to pathogenic

00:09:38.960 --> 00:09:43.120
and antibiotic-resistant E. coli
from retail chicken breasts?

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:48.240
Infections where, though?
The urinary tract.

00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:51.040
There's a type of E. coli
called ST131,

00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:55.520
which is a foodborne
uropathogen, meaning it causes

00:09:55.520 --> 00:09:58.880
urinary tract infections, most of
which are just bladder infections,

00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:02.160
which typically amount to little
more than a painful annoyance,

00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:04.880
but can become invasive and
spread up into the kidneys

00:10:04.880 --> 00:10:08.800
and invade the bloodstream
and end your life.

00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:13.280
E. coli ST131 emerged explosively
in the last 20 years or so

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:16.640
to become the most important
multidrug-resistant uropathogen

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:18.400
in circulation today.

00:10:19.280 --> 00:10:22.000
Urinary tract infections
are caused principally

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:29.280
by ascending E. coli infection via
an intestine-stool-urethra route,

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:33.360
meaning these E. coli that cause
UTIs, called extra-intestinal

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:38.480
pathogenic E. coli or ExPEC
bacteria, start out in the colon,

00:10:38.480 --> 00:10:41.200
make it to the anus and
then make their way

00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:44.720
up into the urethra,
then into your bladder.

00:10:44.720 --> 00:10:48.240
How do they get into your
intestines in the first place?

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:51.360
That's where the chicken comes in.

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:56.000
The role of poultry-meat can be
to introduce the ExPEC bacteria,

00:10:56.000 --> 00:11:00.240
allowing it to colonize in the rectums
of consumers, lying in wait until

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:03.920
an opportunity to cause infection
presents itself, for example,

00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:07.040
thrusting from sexual intercourse
can introduce the bacteria

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:08.720
into the urethra.

00:11:08.720 --> 00:11:13.280
The time lag between human
ExPEC acquisition in the intestine

00:11:13.280 --> 00:11:16.960
and the bladder infection has been
the fundamental challenge

00:11:16.960 --> 00:11:19.440
linking the two, but we now
have strong evidence

00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:22.960
that a substantial portion
of the ST131 strains

00:11:22.960 --> 00:11:26.080
infecting humans
originate from poultry.

00:11:26.080 --> 00:11:29.200
But they couldn't tell whether
any single infection arose

00:11:29.200 --> 00:11:32.480
from direct exposure to
contaminated poultry or

00:11:32.480 --> 00:11:36.240
indirectly from chicken meat from
human-to-human transmission

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:40.880
from, say, a partner who ate
some contaminated poultry.

00:11:40.880 --> 00:11:46.080
What percentage of human
UTIs arise from poultry?

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:48.960
Researchers analyzed E. coli
isolates from urine samples

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:53.120
from patients with suspected UTIs
and compared them to the bacteria

00:11:53.120 --> 00:11:55.920
on retail meat samples
in the same region,

00:11:55.920 --> 00:11:58.400
using DNA fingerprinting techniques.

00:11:58.400 --> 00:12:01.920
They found that about a fifth
of E. coli isolates from suspected

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:06.960
cases of UTIs belonged to types
found in local retail poultry.

00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:11.360
21% might not sound like a lot,
but E. coli UTIs are one of

00:12:11.360 --> 00:12:15.040
the most common infectious
diseases in the United States,

00:12:15.040 --> 00:12:18.480
affecting approximately
seven million women,

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:22.800
so contaminated chicken may
result in more than a million UTIs

00:12:22.800 --> 00:12:25.840
in American women every year.

00:12:25.840 --> 00:12:28.440
This may explain why women
infected with multidrug-resistant

00:12:28.440 --> 00:12:32.160
E. coli reported more frequent
chicken consumption,

00:12:32.160 --> 00:12:35.040
putting them at nearly
four times the odds,

00:12:35.040 --> 00:12:39.600
though frequent consumption
of pork was also a risk factor.

00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:42.800
Wait. Is it found in pigs, too?
Human ExPEC,

00:12:42.800 --> 00:12:46.160
those extraintestinal
E. coli that cause UTIs, have

00:12:46.160 --> 00:12:51.360
also been identified on pig farms,
in pigs, and in retail pork meat,

00:12:51.360 --> 00:12:55.360
albeit at considerably lower levels
than in poultry or chicken meat.

00:12:55.360 --> 00:12:59.920
So chicken is riskiest, pork less so,
and beef could be considered

00:12:59.920 --> 00:13:03.600
the safest from a UTI standpoint,
since cattle don't appear

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:07.280
to be a reservoir of these
particular types of E. coli.

00:13:07.280 --> 00:13:11.200
OK, well, if meat, including poultry
and pork, is the major reservoir

00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:15.840
for these UTI bacteria, then
vegetarians, who avoid meat,

00:13:15.840 --> 00:13:18.480
should theoretically
suffer less exposure.

00:13:18.480 --> 00:13:22.400
However, no study thus far has
examined whether vegetarian diets

00:13:22.400 --> 00:13:27.520
reduce the risk
of UTIs until now:

00:13:27.520 --> 00:13:30.320
a prospective study on the
risk of urinary tract infection

00:13:30.320 --> 00:13:33.600
in vegetarians versus
non-vegetarians.

00:13:33.600 --> 00:13:37.200
Well, if around 20% are
tied to retail chicken meat,

00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:40.560
no surprise that eating vegetarian
is associated with around

00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:44.320
20% lower risk of UTIs,
particularly in women.

00:13:44.320 --> 00:13:47.120
And this association was
independent of diseases

00:13:47.120 --> 00:13:50.160
and predisposing risk factors
like hypertension, diabetes,

00:13:50.160 --> 00:13:52.320
and high cholesterol,
meaning it's not just due

00:13:52.320 --> 00:13:56.080
to the fact that vegetarians
had less diabetes or something.

00:13:56.080 --> 00:13:59.040
What about buying
organic chicken?

00:13:59.040 --> 00:14:01.360
Bacteria swabbed from
chicken labeled organic

00:14:01.360 --> 00:14:05.600
harbored less antibiotic
resistant bacteria, but no --

00:14:05.600 --> 00:14:10.320
no less likely to be contaminated
with ExPEC UTI bacteria.

00:14:10.320 --> 00:14:13.440
These findings suggest that retail
chicken products in the United States,

00:14:13.440 --> 00:14:16.880
even if they're labeled organic,
pose a potential health threat

00:14:16.880 --> 00:14:19.680
to consumers because
they're contaminated with

00:14:19.680 --> 00:14:23.200
extensively antibiotic-resistant
E. coli, including the ones

00:14:23.200 --> 00:14:25.520
that cause UTIs.

00:14:25.520 --> 00:14:30.400
To date only the Jack-in-the-Box
E. coli, like O157:H7,

00:14:30.400 --> 00:14:32.320
are considered food adulterants,

00:14:32.320 --> 00:14:36.800
meaning it's not legal to
knowingly sell contaminated meat.

00:14:36.800 --> 00:14:40.880
Why don't they do the same
with the ExPEC bugs now that

00:14:40.880 --> 00:14:44.160
there's such strong evidence
they're infecting so many women?

00:14:44.160 --> 00:14:46.640
Well, in a survey of retail
chicken breasts collected

00:14:46.640 --> 00:14:48.400
widely across the United States,

00:14:48.400 --> 00:14:52.960
14.3% of the E. coli they found
appeared to be ExPEC.

00:14:52.960 --> 00:14:56.880
Given that E. coli can
be found in about 90%

00:14:56.880 --> 00:14:59.680
of retail turkey and chicken
products, that would mean

00:14:59.680 --> 00:15:00.880
the industry would have to dump

00:15:00.880 --> 00:15:05.360
literally billions of pounds
of chicken breasts every year.

